            "content": [
              {
                "text": "I'm working out the connecting-line rule: same-colored cell pairs get the space between them filled with that color, and when two lines cross, one color takes priority over the other. Looking at the training example, I trace the 5-colored vertical pair spanning rows 3 through 10 to confirm this fill pattern.\n\nChecking more pairs, I see vertical lines consistently override horizontal ones at intersections \u2014 the col7 vertical 5-line overwrites both the 2 and 8 horizontal lines that cross it, while a vertical 1-line at col8 has no crossing conflict to test.\n\nApplying this to the test grid, I identify each colored pair and their fill ranges: a vertical 3-line down col2, a vertical 8-line down col6, a horizontal 2-line across row2 that gets overridden at cols2 and 6 by the verticals, a horizontal 4-line across row6, and a horizontal 1-line across row8 that doesn't intersect either vertical since it's out of range.",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
            ],
